            <div xml:id="d139">

                <pb facs="0225" n="121" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg121"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/3655: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Consul at Geneva (Gilbert)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-01-30T17:00:00-05:00">January 30, 1932—5
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>32. Your 35, January 26, 4 p.m., paragraphs numbered 1 (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >c</hi>) and 4 and 5 and your 49, January 28, 7 p.m.,<anchor n="88"
                        xml:id="d139-f1"/> section 1, concluding paragraphs.</p>
                <p>Supplementing instructions in Department’s 29, January 27, 6 p.m.</p>
                <p>For your guidance, in connection with inquiries as to the attitude of the
                    American Government on the subject of possible invoking of the Nine Power
                    Treaty, you should not initiate any discussion of that question, but in case you
                    should be called upon to answer a responsible inquiry, you may reply in
                    confidence that your Government would without doubt view with satisfaction a
                    declaration by the Council, or by Governments of any of the States represented
                    thereon which are also parties to the Nine Power Treaty, in definite support of
                    the position taken in the American Government’s identic notes of January 7.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
